WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.230
In this video, you configure the backup target in SUSE Virtualization: an S3-compatible bucket that stores virtual machine backups outside the cluster.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:18.870
In Advanced, Settings, the backup-target setting is empty, so backups have nowhere to go yet.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.570
Open its action menu,

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.380
and select Edit Setting.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:29.770
Choose S3 as the type. NFS is the other option.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:36.340
Enter the endpoint of the S3-compatible object store, here a Ceph object gateway.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.410
Enter the name of an existing bucket,

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.400
and its region.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.150
Enter the access key ID of an account that can write to the bucket,

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:49.970
and its secret access key. The field is masked.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:57.190
Keep Virtual Hosted-Style set to False, because this gateway uses path-style bucket URLs.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:00.920
Select Save.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:09.240
Back on the settings list, the setting is tagged Modified. Select Test connection to check that the bucket is reachable.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.840
The connection test succeeds.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.160
Select Show backup-target to review the saved value.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:24.390
It lists the endpoint, bucket, and region. The keys are kept separately in a Kubernetes secret.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:32.107
The backup target is configured. Next, you back up a virtual machine and restore it from that backup.
